# Frequent Directions merge topology: source and mathematics audit

## Why this is a new serial question

The w010 Nyström/RFF comparison was retired because its proposed equal-memory
frontier depended on implementation-specific state accounting.  Frequent
Directions (FD) instead exposes an intrinsic summary parameter: an `ell x d`
matrix, with temporary shrink storage reported separately.  This question does
not reopen the retired consistent-LRA update, Block-Krylov, or Nyström/RFF
branches.

## Primary-source facts

The fixed primary source is Ghashami, Liberty, Phillips and Woodruff,
*Frequent Directions: Simple and Deterministic Matrix Sketching*, arXiv
1501.01711v2 (2015), especially Algorithm 1, Theorems 1.1--1.2 and Section 3.1.
The source defines an `ell x d` deterministic sketch, proves covariance and
rank-k projection bounds, and calls the summary mergeable.  Section 3.1 forms
`B'=[B1;B2]`, applies FD once more, and proves that the merged sketch satisfies
the same three proof properties with

`Delta = Delta_1 + Delta_2 + delta_merge`.

It states that partitions may be combined in arbitrary order.  The proof gives
the same formal space/error trade-off; it does not state that different merge
trees produce identical sketches, equal discarded mass, equal realized
projection error, or equal runtime.  The 2016 Sparse Frequent Directions paper
also uses repeated merge/shrink steps and identifies exact SVD shrink cost as a
runtime bottleneck.  Therefore a claim that FD is newly mergeable would be a
direct collision with prior work.  The open scoped question here is only the
finite implementation quality/cost sensitivity within the established guarantee.

