## Exact-arithmetic accounting

For a binary merge tree `T` over fixed leaf sketches, let `Delta_i` be the
discarded-mass sum inside leaf `i`, and let `delta_v` be the singular-value
threshold at internal merge node `v`.  Repeatedly applying the source proof gives

`Delta_T = sum_i Delta_i + sum_v delta_v`.

For the exact shrink used here, each shrink subtracts the same `delta` from
`ell` squared singular values, so

`||A||_F^2 - ||B_T||_F^2 = ell * Delta_T`

up to floating-point roundoff.  Thus the total discarded mass is independently
recoverable from input and final sketch Frobenius norms.  Different trees may
have different internal `delta_v` values, hence different `Delta_T` and realized
errors, without violating mergeability.

## Decision boundary

The next experiment may test a topology heuristic, not a new FD guarantee:
on the already retained 512-row Landmark prefix, with fixed contiguous leaves,
does a balanced tree reduce cumulative discarded mass and rank-25 projection
error relative to a left-deep merge at the same exported `ell x d` summary?
Central row-stream FD and exact rank-25 SVD are required controls.  Any violation
of the declared all-width direction retires the monotone topology heuristic;
all formal FD guarantees remain intact.  A positive bounded result would still
require new data/partition confirmation and closest-work review before any method
claim.
